# DataAnalytics-L2-Task2-WineQualityPrediction

## Wine Quality Prediction

### Objective

The objective of this project is to classify wines into Low, Medium, and High quality categories using their physicochemical properties and machine learning classification algorithms.

## Dataset

The Wine Quality dataset contains physicochemical measurements of red wine samples and their quality scores.

The original quality scores range from 3 to 8.

### Feature Engineering

The original quality scores were converted into three classes:

- Low: Quality 3–4
- Medium: Quality 5–6
- High: Quality 7–8

## Data Preprocessing

The following steps were performed:

- Dataset inspection
- Missing-value analysis
- Duplicate-value analysis
- Feature distribution analysis
- Correlation analysis
- Conversion of quality scores into three classes
- Stratified train-test split
- Feature scaling for SGD and SVC

## Models Used

Three classification algorithms were trained:

1. Random Forest
2. SGD Classifier
3. Support Vector Classifier (SVC)

## Model Performance

| Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1-Score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Random Forest | 0.8688 | 0.8275 | 0.8688 | 0.8458 |
| SGD Classifier | 0.7781 | 0.7929 | 0.7781 | 0.7848 |
| SVC | 0.7094 | 0.8150 | 0.7094 | 0.7448 |

## Best Model

Random Forest achieved the best overall performance with:

- Accuracy: **86.88%**
- Precision: **82.75%**
- Recall: **86.88%**
- F1-Score: **84.58%**

Therefore, Random Forest was selected as the best-performing model.

## Visualizations

The project includes:

- Wine quality distribution
- Chemical feature distributions
- Correlation heatmap
- Quality class distribution
- Confusion matrices
- Random Forest feature importance
- Model performance comparison

## Conclusion

Machine learning can effectively classify wine quality using physicochemical properties.

Among the three evaluated models, Random Forest performed best, achieving approximately 86.88% accuracy and an F1-score of 84.58%.

The class imbalance was addressed by using stratified sampling and balanced Random Forest training. Feature-importance analysis also provides insight into which chemical properties contribute most to wine-quality classification.

The model could potentially support automated wine quality assessment and quality-control processes in the wine industry.

